{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TfG6AmZE5j5Z2WqNRQFwbJ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→{\n     2→  \"metadata\": {\n     3→    \"title\": \"Masque of the Red Death - Poe\",\n     4→    \"author\": \"Edgar Allan Poe\",\n     5→    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/Masque of the Red Death - Poe.txt\",\n     6→    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n     7→    \"total_word_count\": 2449,\n     8→    \"total_character_count\": 13811,\n     9→    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 16.286666666666665,\n    10→    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n    11→  },\n    12→  \"structure\": [\n    13→    {\n    14→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    15→      \"title\": null,\n    16→      \"index\": 1,\n    17→      \"start_position\": 28,\n    18→      \"end_position\": 13811,\n    19→      \"word_count\": 2443,\n    20→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 16.286666666666665,\n    21→      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n    22→      \"children\": [],\n    23→      \"characters_present\": [\n    24→        \"Prince Prospero\",\n    25→        \"The masked figure (Red Death)\",\n    26→        \"Courtiers\",\n    27→        \"Musicians\",\n    28→        \"Buffoons\",\n    29→        \"Improvisatori\",\n    30→        \"Ballet-dancers\",\n    31→        \"Beauty\"\n    32→      ],\n    33→      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with the devastating spread of the 'Red Death,' a horrific plague marked by sharp pains, profuse bleeding, and scarlet stains that isolate victims from society. Prince Prospero, in defiance of the plague, retreats with a thousand courtiers to his secluded, fortified abbey, where he provides endless entertainment and barricades all exits to ensure safety. Months later, he hosts a lavish masked ball in seven uniquely colored rooms, each lit by blood-red firelight through stained-glass windows, culminating in a black chamber with scarlet panes and a massive ebony clock that halts all activity with its hourly chime. When a mysterious figure dressed as the Red Death appears—dripping in blood and wearing a corpse-like mask—the courtiers are paralyzed by terror, and the prince, enraged, pursues the intruder through all seven rooms with a dagger, only to collapse dead upon confronting the figure, who is revealed to be the plague itself; one by one, all revelers die as the clock stops and darkness claims the abbey.\"\n    34→    }\n    35→  ],\n    36→  \"characters\": [\n    37→    {\n    38→      \"id\": \"char_the_prince_prospero_d85685e9\",\n    39→      \"canonical_name\": \"the Prince Prospero\",\n    40→      \"aliases\": [\n    41→        \"Prospero\",\n    42→        \"the mummer\"\n    43→      ],\n    44→      \"role\": null,\n    45→      \"appearance\": null,\n    46→      \"personality\": null,\n    47→      \"voice_guidance\": null,\n    48→      \"descriptions\": [\n    49→        {\n    50→          \"text\": \"Prince Prospero is a self-indulgent and tyrannical ruler who isolates himself and a thousand courtiers in a fortified abbey to escape the plague, prioritizing his own safety and pleasure over the lives of others. His actions—sealing the abbey, hosting a lavish ball during a deadly epidemic, and violently pursuing a masked figure—reveal a man consumed by arrogance, fear, and a desire to assert control, ultimately leading to his death when he confronts the embodiment of the plague he sought to evade.\",\n    51→          \"source_position\": 1894,\n    52→          \"chapter_index\": null,\n    53→          \"confidence\": \"llm_refined\"\n    54→        }\n    55→      ],\n    56→      \"relationships\": {},\n    57→      \"evidence\": [\n    58→        {\n    59→          \"statement\": \"Prince Prospero isolates himself and a thousand courtiers in a fortified abbey to escape the plague\",\n    60→          \"quote\": \"Prince Prospero, in defiance of the plague, retreats with a thousand courtiers to his secluded, fortified abbey, where he provides endless entertainment a